Reserving Compute Capacity for Applications
Objectives
Configure an application with resource requests so Kubernetes can make scheduling decisions.

1. Pod Scheduling Overview
Kubernetes uses the scheduler to place pods onto nodes. This ensures workloads get the compute resources they need and prevents resource contention.
Scheduler steps:
1.	Filtering nodes (predicates):
o	Considers only nodes matching pod requirements, like node labels or resource requests (CPU, memory).
o	If no node meets the requirements → FailedScheduling event.
2.	Prioritizing nodes (priorities):
o	Eligible nodes are scored based on various criteria. Higher score = better candidate.
3.	Selecting a node:
o	Node with highest score is chosen. Ties are resolved round-robin.
o	Pod is scheduled → Scheduled event is generated.
Note: Scheduler can be customized for advanced needs (not covered here).
________________________________________
2. Configuring Compute Resource Requests
Why:
•	Requests reserve minimum CPU/memory for pods → scheduler places pods only on nodes that can satisfy the request.
Units:
•	CPU: in millicores (m) → 1000 m = 1 CPU core.
o	Example: 0.1 CPU = 100m
•	Memory: in bytes, e.g., 1Gi = 1 gibibyte.
Example in YAML:
spec:
  containers:
  - image: quay.io/redhattraining/hello-world-nginx:v1.0
    name: hello-world-nginx
    resources:
      requests:
        cpu: "100m"
        memory: "1Gi"
Command-line equivalent:
oc set resources deployment hello-world-nginx --requests cpu=100m,memory=1Gi
Explanation:
•	Ensures scheduler only runs pod on a node with ≥100 m CPU and 1Gi memory available.
