3. Inspecting Cluster Resources
View node capacity and allocations:
oc describe node master01
•	Capacity: Total CPU, memory, storage on the node.
•	Allocatable: Resources available for pods after system overhead.
•	Non-terminated pods: Shows CPU/memory requests and limits for each running pod.
Check actual pod usage (per project):
oc adm top pods -n <project-name>
# Example:
oc adm top pods -n openshift-dns
Check node resource usage:
oc adm top nodes
# or specific node:
oc adm top node master01
Theory:
•	Helps admins see which nodes are overcommitted or underutilized.
•	Requests + limits help prevent resource starvation for important workloads.
________________________________________
4. Key Points / Best Practices
•	Resource requests: minimum guaranteed resources; scheduler uses these to place pods.
•	Resource limits: maximum allowed usage; prevents pods from hogging node resources.
•	Always define requests and limits for production apps to ensure HA and fair scheduling.
•	Use oc describe node and oc adm top to monitor actual usage.
•	Millicores allow fractional CPU allocation, useful in multi-tenant clusters.
________________________________________
5. Quick Commands Summary
Task	Command
Set CPU & memory requests	oc set resources deployment <deployment-name> --requests cpu=100m,memory=1Gi
View node details	oc describe node <node-name>
Show pod resource usage in a project	oc adm top pods -n <project-name>
Show node resource usage	oc adm top nodes
